# First Aid Form Upgrade: 2025/26 Season Analysis

**Source:** First Aid Form Data.xlsx (OneDrive inbox, as at 9 August 2026)
**Method:** All figures re-derived from the raw season sheets (20-21 through 25-26_NEW) using the scripts in `analysis/`. The colleague-crunched sheets (Year 2 Year, Stats (25-26), By Unit, Resus) were treated as claims to reconcile, not as sources. Every 2025/26 figure in those sheets that was tested reconciled exactly; the prior-season formulas in Year 2 Year are broken and corrected figures appear below.
**Scope discipline:** Nothing in this document relies on information outside the workbook. Limitations are stated at the end.

---

## 1. Executive summary

The 2025/26 season replaced a flat first aid form with a conditional (branching) form. Six seasons of raw data (7,406 cases) support four conclusions.

1. **The new form measures things the old form structurally could not.** Documentation delay, completion state, clinical timelines for resuscitation, analgesia audit trails, patient outcomes and responder welfare are all visible for the first time. None of these were recoverable from the old form's data model.
2. **The branching design works.** When a conditional question set is presented, it is answered 96.6 to 100 per cent of the time. Unanswered questions concentrate almost entirely in forms still open in the workflow, not in completed forms.
3. **Documentation is fast and timely.** Half of all forms reach their last edit within about 4 minutes of creation. 87.1 per cent of cases are documented within one hour of treatment. 95.0 per cent of forms reached completed status.
4. **The data now identifies its own improvement targets.** Free-text overflow rates isolate exactly which option lists need work, and capture-rate gaps (SAMPLE history, date of birth, ambulance case numbers) define the season's training priorities.

