## 3. Clarity the new form created (things the old form could not tell us)

### 3.1 Documentation timeliness is measurable for the first time

The old form's date field mirrors the record creation time (in 2024/25, every computable treatment-to-creation delta is approximately zero or slightly negative, and only 460 of 1,688 rows carry the field at all). Time from treatment to documentation was therefore unmeasurable. The new form records treatment time independently.

| Measure (2025/26 new form, n=1,494) | Result |
|---|---|
| Cases documented within 1 hour of treatment | 1,301 (87.1%) |
| Within 3 hours | 93.4% |
| Documented more than 24 hours after treatment | 41 (2.7%) |
| Major incidents documented within 1 hour | 77.1% (crews are treating first) |
| Minor incidents documented within 1 hour | 89.1% |

### 3.2 Completion state is visible

Old-form exports show 100 per cent of records as completed, because the old workflow had no visible open state. The new workflow exposes it: 1,420 of 1,494 forms (95.0 per cent) completed, 74 open, and reopen reasons are logged (the recorded reasons are administrative: linking to incidents, reclassification, comms testing). Median time from creation to last edit is approximately 4 minutes; 85.4 per cent of forms are finalised within an hour of being opened.

### 3.3 Structured data replaced a free-text catch-all

96.4 per cent of 2024/25 cases carried a free-text narrative, because the notes box was where clinical detail had to live. Under the new form only 15.6 per cent of cases need a narrative; the same information now lands in queryable fields (specific problem, body location, treatments, discharge pathway, outcome). This is the difference that makes every other analysis in this document possible.

### 3.4 Resuscitation cases now carry a clinical timeline

All 7 resuscitation-branch cases in 2025/26 (all classified major) record 11 of 12 detail fields: equipment used, initial airway assessment, method, time of first compression, defibrillator time, CPR cycles, first AED rhythm, shock count, return of breathing, and pulse. The only gap is the barriers field, which the change log already plans to make optional. Under the old form, only 14 of 28 comparable 2024/25 cases (50 per cent) carried any resuscitation detail at all.

### 3.5 Analgesia has an audit trail

Methoxyflurane administration now records vial numbers, dose times, administering member, contraindications, adverse effects and serial pain ratings. In 2025/26 there are 14 branch records. Among the 8 cases with a starting pain score above zero, mean initial pain was 7.1 out of 10, falling by a mean of 2.6 points within 4 minutes of administration (n=7 with 4-minute scores). The old form recorded roughly 20 Penthrane administrations in 2024/25 as a bare treatment tag with no structured record. Oxygen therapy likewise now has 47 administration records (therapy mask 37, bag valve mask 5, oxygen tubing 3).

### 3.6 Outcomes and welfare are recorded

- Condition at discharge answered on 98 per cent of completed forms (1,417 conscious, 4 deceased, 2 unconscious).
- Patient condition after treatment recorded on 263 cases; all 3 documented deteriorations include the mechanism.
- Hospital pathway (220 cases): ambulance handover documented on 119, destination hospital named on 66, ambulance case number captured on 18.
- Responder welfare questions are asked for the first time: 7 peer-support requests and 1 occupational-violence report surfaced in season one.

## 4. Trends in first aid presentations

- **Volume:** 673 to 1,518 cases across six seasons (+126 per cent). Like-for-like season windows put 2025/26 3.6 per cent above 2024/25.
- **Abrasions and grazes** climbed steadily as a share of old-form cases: 17.4% (20/21), 27.6%, 27.3%, 29.5%, 31.8% (24/25). Lacerations held near 21 per cent throughout.
- **Marine stings are episodic, not trending:** 15.5 per cent of cases in 2021/22 against 6.8 to 8.8 per cent in other old-form seasons. In 2025/26, envenomation is 13.7 per cent of cases (204), and the new taxonomy attributes them at species level (jellyfish 151, bee 14, other insects, stingray 4).
- **Heat presentations rose across five old-form seasons:** heat stroke and heat exhaustion grew from 0.7 per cent of cases (20/21) to 4.1 per cent (24/25).
- **Severity gradient by nature (2025/26):** suspected drowning 91.7 per cent major, resuscitation 100 per cent, mental health 4 of 5, medical 46.4 per cent, environmental 25.0 per cent, injury 14.6 per cent, envenomation 3.4 per cent. This is the evidence base for matching skill requirements to presentation type.
- **Demographics are stable and young:** median patient age 16, 53.9 per cent under 18, the 10 to 14 band is the largest (330 cases). Gender is balanced (767 M, 720 F).
- **Load concentrates:** January carries 43.6 per cent of new-form season cases, weekends 51.7 per cent, and the 15:00 hour is the daily peak. 126 units logged cases; the top ten units account for a third of the load, led by Aquatic Sports (168).
- **Ambulance involvement:** 9.7 per cent of 2025/26 cases had an ambulance called; 8.4 per cent discharged into an ambulance pathway, consistent with prior seasons (7.8 to 14.3 per cent).

## 5. Form performance (the James questions)

### 5.1 Time from opening to completion

Median creation-to-last-edit is approximately 4 minutes (p25 2 minutes, p75 12 minutes). 85.4 per cent finish inside an hour. The long tail (120 forms with more than 24 hours) is materially administrative: reopen reasons in the data are incident linking, log attachment and reclassification, not slow first-pass documentation. This distribution matches the By Unit sheet's bucket analysis within method differences.

### 5.2 Question sets answered well

Every conditional branch performs when presented: specific medical problem 100 per cent (138/138), injury 99.3 per cent (1,011/1,018), mental health 5/5, drowning 12/12, environmental 98.3 per cent, envenomation 96.6 per cent. Injury body location is present on 97.2 per cent of injury cases. Core categorical blanks on completed forms are 1.7 to 2.1 per cent. No malformed duplicate multiselect values exist in the core fields of the current export (the multiselect artefact flagged on the notes sheet did not reproduce in these columns).

### 5.3 Question sets answered poorly (training and design targets)

| Question set | Capture (2025/26) | Note |
|---|---|---|
| SAMPLE history (past medical history / allergies / medications) | 7.0% / 7.2% / 6.0% overall; 12.9% / 9.6% / 8.8% on majors | Matches the notes sheet's training flag. Low even where it matters most. |
| Full name | FullName 57.8%, FirstOnly 28.2% | Not-captured halved against the old form (see 5.4), but first-name-only grew. Rename to "Full Name" is on the change list. |
| Date of birth | 11.2% (was 34.0% in 2024/25) | Age is captured instead (98.6%). A deliberate decision is needed on whether DOB matters. |
| Address / nationality / postcode | 6.6% / 6.5% / 81.3% (down from 18.1% / 34.9% / 94.9%) | Consistent with these moving to an optional question group. |
| Ambulance case number | 18 of 220 hospital-pathway cases (8.2%) | Small effort, high value for continuity of care. |
| Patient condition after treatment | 28.9% on majors | Outcome question worth reinforcing for major incidents. |
| Care participants | 42 of 1,494 (2.8%) | Rarely used. |

### 5.4 Capture rates, old form against new form

| Field | 2024/25 (old) | 2025/26 (new) | Direction |
|---|---|---|---|
| Any patient name | 72.1% | 86.0% | Up; not-captured halved from 27.9% to 14.0% |
| Age | 99.9% | 98.6% | Held |
| Gender | 100% | 100% | Held |
| Phone | not available | 10.0% | New capability |
| Date of birth | 34.0% | 11.2% | Down (age captured instead) |
| Postcode | 94.9% | 81.3% | Down |
| Address | 18.1% | 6.6% | Down |
| Nationality | 34.9% | 6.5% | Down |

The demographic declines are concentrated in fields the new design treats as optional. The honest framing for executives: the new form trades low-value demographic completeness for near-perfect clinical completeness, and the one identity field that operationally matters (name) improved materially. If DOB, address or nationality are organisationally required, that is a form-design decision, not a member-performance problem.

### 5.5 Option lists that need work (data-driven form iteration)

Free-text "Other" overflow by branch: nature 1.7 per cent, injury 1.1 per cent, medical 10.1 per cent, envenomation 13.6 per cent, environmental 30.5 per cent. The environmental and envenomation lists are missing common options (overflow text includes hypothermia-adjacent, insect and heat entries). The notes sheet's planned changes (SpO2 field, barriers optional, renames) show the loop from data to design already operating.

## 6. Conference statistics (the wow list)

1. Clean cutover in three days: last old form 9 November 2025, first new form 12 November 2025; 1,494 new-form cases in season one with no parallel running.
2. A richer form did not slow anyone down: median 4 minutes from opening to final edit, 95.0 per cent completion.
3. 87.1 per cent of cases documented within one hour of treatment, a number that could not be measured before this season.
4. Conditional questions are answered 96.6 to 100 per cent of the time they are presented.
5. Unidentified patients halved (27.9 per cent to 14.0 per cent).
6. Every resuscitation now carries a near-complete clinical timeline (11 of 12 fields on 7 of 7 cases); the old form managed any detail on half of comparable cases.
7. Vial-level analgesia auditability: mean pain 7.1 falling 2.6 points within 4 minutes across recorded methoxyflurane cases.
8. The form now surfaces responder welfare: 7 peer-support requests in season one that previously had no channel.
9. Form improvement is now empirical: overflow rates name the exact option lists to fix.
10. All of this at 126 per cent volume growth across six seasons, with January alone carrying 43.6 per cent of the season.

## 7. Limitations (what this data cannot support)

- The workbook contains no user-satisfaction survey. Satisfaction claims must rest on behavioural evidence only: completion rate, completion speed, adoption, and branch answer rates.
- Resuscitation (7) and methoxyflurane (14) counts are small; report them as counts, not percentages with implied precision.
- Old and new taxonomies differ; cross-form trend comparisons are restricted to shared or mapped categories.
- Timestamps measure documentation behaviour, not care delivery. The completion long tail includes administrative reopens.
- The 2024/25 export spans all calendar months; season-on-season volume comparisons use the matched November-to-June window stated in section 2.
- Four treatment-to-creation deltas are negative (data-entry anomalies) and were excluded from timeliness claims.

